## Introduction
Photosynthetic organisms convert sunlight to energy-dense compounds such as sugars, from which energy can be extracted at any time of need, even in the absence of sunlight. This function of photosynthesis is being mimicked with synthetic materials in a process termed artificial photosynthesis. Artificial photosynthesis, broadly defined here as the conversion of sunlight or solar-derived electricity into chemical fuels, mimics several functions as natural photosynthesis, including (1) light absorption and charge separation (Cardona et al. 2012) and (2) chemical catalysis (Cox et al. 2013). Photosynthesis accomplishes light absorption and charge separation in photosystems I and II prior to generating NADPH and ATP as ubiquitous energy carriers. These carriers are then used to power the Calvin cycle and other metabolic processes that build energy-dense units from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ building blocks (Fig. 1A). Artificial approaches either (1) couple light absorption and chemical catalysis within a single system (e.g. photocatalysts or photoelectrochemical cells) or (2) decouple light absorption and chemical catalysis (e.g. in photovoltaic-electrolyzer systems; Fig. 1B). Light absorption and charge separation are now readily accomplished with semiconductor materials which, in contrast to the chlorophylls used in photosynthesis, possess high absorption coefficients across a broad energy range for potentially highly efficient solar energy harvesting (Blankenship et al. 2011). However, mimicking nature’s catalytic chemistry in
