generating chemical fuels from water oxidation and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation remains a substantial challenge using synthetic catalysts in either artificial photosynthesis approach.
In the context of electrochemical fuel-forming reactions, a basic understanding has been developed regarding the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), and a number of highly efficient, cost effective, and stable HER catalysts have been developed(McKone et al. 2014, Roger et al. 2017). In contrast, catalyst development towards the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathbf{R}$ ) to carbon-based fuels and chemicals lags far behind. This is primarily due to the sluggish kinetics of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, the possibility of multiple reaction pathways, and the need for selectivity towards reactions with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ instead of HER. A wide array of catalysts has been investigated towards the utilization of electricity to reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to fuels and chemicals. Primary classes of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ catalysts are molecular and heterogeneous inorganic materials, each of which possess key strengths and weaknesses. Molecular catalysts can be tuned to probe effects of binding sites and ligands, yet these materials are not always soluble and are often prone to degradation. Heterogeneous catalysts (e.g. nanoparticles) typically exhibit higher stabilities and activities, though are not as amenable towards modification at a molecular level.
Recently, a class of hybrid $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ catalysts has emerged in the form of metal and covalent organic frameworks (MOFs and COFs). MOFs and COFs are comprised of organic units arranged in a threedimensional porous structure. Such materials not only combine the strengths of molecular and heterogeneous catalytic materials but also offer unique avenues for catalysis within the confined dimensions of their reaction environment, similarly to how enzymes take advantage of the entirety of their catalytic pocket. As such, this mini review will first examine MOFs and COFs used as electrocatalysts for hydrogen generation and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ , then offer several directions, including prospects in material design and spectroscopic analysis, which can help understand and take full advantage of the unique aspects of MOFs and COFs in precisely controlling chemical reactivity within their catalytic pockets. Several excellent reviews on MOF- and COF-based electrocatalysis have been published (Downes and Marinescu 2017b, Al-Rowaili et al. 2018, Lei et al. 2018, Lin et al. 2018, Zhang et al. 2018) and, within this framework, we aim to offer our complementary perspective on the emerging field by offering prospective directions in material design and spectroscopic analysis. MOFs also have many applications in gas phase catalysis such as methanol generation from methane which are highly relevant towards natural gas utilization (Baek et al. 2018). While the focus of this work will be on MOFs and COFs in the context of electrocatalysis,
