## MOF and COF interfaces and electrochemistry
The use of MOFs and COFs as electrocatalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction carries a few straightforward requirements. Electrons must transfer from a conductive electrode to the MOF/COF, then through the MOF/COF structure to a catalytically active site (Fig. 2A). Charge transport through the framework can either proceed via a hopping mechanism through redox-active units and protonation/deprotonation of chemical species or through band-like conduction in conductive channels (Fig. 2B). $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and a source of protons, commonly $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or bicarbonate, must also be able to transfer from the electrolyte to the active site and be selectively reduced to the desired product. In addition, ions in the electrolyte must be able to move within the MOF structure to counterbalance positive or negative charges within the framework. A full understanding of the interplay of all these processes within an electrocatalyst framework must still be developed to enable the rational design of MOF and COF electrocatalytic systems.
MOF films can be directly deposited on substrates from pre-formed, solvothermally-grown MOF powders, or by in situ solvothermal growth from reactant solutions containing the metal precursor and/or the organic linker. Despite their simplicity, these methods tend to result in large crystallites of varying size and orientation and offer limited control of film thickness. Alternatively, liquid phase epitaxy (LPE) can be used, whereby films are grown in an indirect, layer-by-layer (LbL) fashion from the different film components, e.g. through sequential immersion in solutions of the metal precursor and organic linker (So et al. 2013). LPE tends to result in continuous, well-defined, and orientated films, with controllable film thickness, and furthermore allows the possibility to deposit heterostructures with chemically different layers (Shekhah et al. 2011). In a similar manner, atomic layer deposition (ALD) and chemical vapor deposition (CVD) can be used, where film growth occurs from gaseous precursors (Lausund and Nilsen 2016, Stassen et al. 2016). The properties of MOF films, such as orientation, thickness and the presence of defects, can greatly influence their electrocatalytic performances, and as such it may be highly desirable to use LbL methods to deposit films for rationally-designed framework catalyst films.
