Suitable functional groups must be present on the surface of the substrate to allow for the anchoring of film components/metal clusters or organic ligands, thus allowing nucleation of MOF films. These functional groups may already be present on the native surface of the substrate (e.g. –OH groups on oxide surfaces), or may be introduced via different functionalization strategies, depending on the type of substrate used. Certain molecules spontaneously adsorb onto substrates from the liquid or vapor phase resulting in self-assembled monolayers (SAMs). Typically, these molecules chemisorb onto the surface via an anchoring group and are separated from the terminal functional group by a spacer/molecular bridge, with the intermolecular interactions between the molecules also provide a driving force for interface formation (Love et al. 2005). Charge transfer across the interface to an electroactive species occurs via tunnelling, with the charge transfer rate given by: $k_{\mathrm{ET}}=A e^{\sqrt{\beta d}}$ , where $d$ is the distance across which tunneling occurs, $A$ is a pre-exponential factor that (amongst other things) depends on the electronic coupling, and $\beta$ is a decay constant that depends on the nature of the spacer/molecular bridge (Chen et al. 2006). Different anchoring groups give different electronic couplings and contact resistances, while conjugated chain systems tend to afford significantly lower decay constants than saturated aliphatic chains (Chen et al. 2006).
Anchoring groups commonly used for depositing SAMs and interfaces on surfaces include: thiols on metals, silanes, phosphonic acids, phosphonate esters, carboxylic acids, hydroxamic acids and other organic anchoring groups on oxide materials, and terminal alkynes and alkenes on hydrogen-terminated Si (Pujari et al. 2014). Hydrolytic and electrochemical stability should be taken into consideration as well as interfacial charge transfer (ICT). A disruption of the interfacial bonds at the electrode surface or a restructuring of the interface SAM may negatively affect ICT, and lead to delamination of any attached film. For example, carboxylic acid anchoring on oxides has been shown to facilitate high ICT rates due to strong electronic coupling at the interface but the anchors readily desorb at $\mathrm{pH}$ greater than 4 (Bae et al. 2004). Conversely, phosphonic acid and silane-derived anchoring groups offer stabilities that are orders of magnitude higher, but this stability comes at the expense of slower ICT rates due to weaker electronic coupling (Gillaizeau-Gauthier et al. 2001). Meanwhile, thiol SAMs on Au facilitate fast ICT and are stable in a broad pH range. However, despite fairly good stabilities under applied cathodic potentials, they will desorb under moderate anodic potentials (Harris et al. 2018a). To date, surface-supported MOF films
