have mainly been grown on thiol SAMs on Au, or using carboxylic acid and silane anchoring groups on oxide materials (Ruiz et al. 2013).
Alternative functionalization strategies that result in highly stable interfaces include the photochemical grafting of alkenes on oxides surfaces such as ITO (Li and Zuilhof 2012), $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (Benson et al. 2011) and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Franking et al. 2009), as well as the electrochemical grafting of diazonium salts (Pinson and Podvorica 2005). Methods for suppressing often-observed multilayer formation during electrografting have been developed, and fast ICT can be achieved, possibly due to improved electronic coupling or the conjugated nature of the interface (Harris et al. 2018b). Indeed, the layer-by-layer growth of electrochemically addressable MFM-186 and MFM-180 MOF films was recently demonstrated on both ITO and carbon paper with –COOH terminated interfaces created through the electrografting of diazonium salts (Mitra et al. 2018).
More recently, conductive MOFs and COFs grown on electrode surfaces were studied with electroanalytical and spectroelectrochemical techniques in electrochemical cells. Such studies are important to gauge the functional conductivity of MOFs and COFs for applications in electrocatalysis. A linear relationship of the peak current of a redox-active unit vs the square root of the scan rate provided evidence for a hopping mechanism of conductivity in a metalloporphyrin MOF thin film (Ahrenholtz et al. 2014). Bias- and time-dependent changes in the absorbance of MOF films allowed for the extraction of the redox potential of the cobalt porphyrin unit and the apparent electron diffusion coefficient of 7.55 $\times10^{-14}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ within the film. Another study incorporated redox active naphthalene diimide linkers and proton-responsive OH groups into a UIO-type MOF thin film to render it conductive. Spectroelectrochemical analysis revealed that charge transport occurred via linker-to-linker hopping, balanced by ion transport from the electrolyte. Changing the electrolyte cation from $n{\mathrm{-Bu_{4}N P F_{6}}}$ to $\mathrm{KPF}_{6}$ increased the effective diffusion coefficient from $4.0{\times}10^{-12}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ to $5.4{\times}10^{-11}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , highlighting the effects of ion diffusion through the film on the resultant conductivity (Johnson et al. 2018). While these diffusion coefficients are quite low, the use of MOF and COF thin films on the order of ${\sim}1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ alleviates these conductivity limitations. All in all, these studies provide a valuable framework for the design and future evaluation of the conductivity of MOF and COF electrocatalysts as well as the necessary factors to accelerate their performance.
