## MOF and COF electrochemical hydrogen evolution and oxygen evolution reactions
The HER and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) are suitable model reactions in which the utility of various MOFs/COFs can be explored because (1) there is no need for selectivity as $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{O}_{2}$ are typically the only possible products, and (2) $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ produced from water electrolysis is a chemical fuel. Key to efficient HER and OER reactivity is a catalytic system with highly active reaction sites for catalysis and a high density of such sites. The active sites must be well-connected to the electrode and accessible to reactants from the bulk electrolyte. Utilizing well-dispersed and isolated catalytic sites in MOFs has been exemplified through MOFs featuring catalytically active polyoxometalates as their nodes (Qin et al. 2015). These materials benefitted from a high degree of accessibility to their active sites exhibited stability in both acidic and basic media. A similar concept has been harnessed with 2D MOFs, which feature isolated and catalytically active Ni sites with an open coordination environment that allows for the efficient reduction of $_\mathrm{H}\mathrm{_2O}$ to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (Dong et al. 2015). A study on a similar set of materials bearing Co sites revealed the importance of reactant accessibility, as the electrodes with a higher electrochemically active surface area, as measured through double layer capacitance measurements, exhibited higher HER activities (Downes and Marinescu 2017a). Another design consideration towards electrocatalytic MOFs and COFs is optimizing the balance of active sites, which are increased with film thickness, and mass and charge transfer, which follow the opposite trend. To this end, a recent investigation utilizing voltammetry, capacitance, and impedance measurements found that Co-benzene hexatiolate frameworks thicknesses of ${\sim}250~\mathrm{nm}$ offered the best compromise between these factors and resulted in the highest performing systems (Downes et al. 2018). A Cu-based MOF grown onto graphene also exhibited high HER activity in acidic media (Jahan et al. 2013). This system benefited from the graphene’s high conductivity and surface area, allowing for exceptional active exposure of the Cu active sites, which were electrically well connected to the conductive support.
In MOF catalysts, subtle changes in ligation can play a large role in the resultant activity of the active sites, as previously demonstrated in catalysis with molecular catalysts (Thoi et al. 2013). Within this context, a recent study demonstrated that two Co-based MOF polymorphs, one with water coordinated to the cobalt center and another with water molecules only hydrogen-bonding within the MOF cavities,
