featured distinctly different HER activity in acidic electrolyte (Wu et al. 2017) with the former being more active. The differences were, in part, related to the different electronic structure of the Co active sites in the MOFs. Inspired by the active sites of molecular and even enzymatic catalysts, MOFs with HER-active cobaloxime units (Roy et al. 2018) and [Fe-Fe] hydrogenase active site mimics (Pullen et al. 2017) have recently been developed.
In a study of MOFs utilized towards the OER, coordinatively unsaturated Ni and Co atoms stabilized on the surfaces of MOF thin films exhibited higher activities than the atoms within the bulk (Zhao et al. 2016). A conceptually similar work entailed the use of MOFs with low crystallinity and hence, a plethora of active sites for efficient OER catalysis (Li et al. 2018b). Taking advantage of the modularity of MOFs through a post-synthetic approach, inert Co-Cl species lining the interior of the MOF pores were exchanged to catalytically active Co-OH units, resulting in an efficient MOF OER catalyst (Lu et al. 2016). Similar to the best performing heterogeneous OER catalysts, mixed-metal (Co, Fe, Ni) oxides, efficient MOF-based OER catalysts also routinely feature a mixture of the same transition metal in their active sites or nodes (Li et al. 2018a, Zhao et al. 2018). The strengths of MOFs here come into play as they can be designed to feature well-dispersed combinations of such elements in their linkers or nodes.
These examples highlight the abilities of MOFs and COFs to not only feature a high density of atomically defined active sites within a three-dimensional architecture, but also to enhance their activity via unique coordination. In a complementary role, a hydroxyl functionalized NU-1000 MOF was found to enhance the catalytic activity of the electrode it was grown on by acting as a proton relay to the electrode catalytic sites (Hod et al. 2015b), highlighting the possibility of MOFs to also accelerate catalysis through modulation of reactant and/or product diffusion.
## MOF and COF electrochemical $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ fixation
Similar concepts as those above have been applied to generate MOFs and COFs that are active towards the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to carbon-based fuels and chemicals. Within the context of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ , MOFs and COFs are attractive because they can utilize molecularly-defined $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ catalysts, which often possess high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ selectivity. The molecular linkers, the MOF/COF porosity, as well as thickness, electrode design,
