and electrolyte composition can be systematically tuned to optimize the performance metric(s) of interest (Fig. 3A).
Initial works first utilized open metal sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathbf{R}$ catalysis. Two Cu-based MOFs, in which $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was presumably bound to the open Cu sites of the MOF nodes, reduced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formate (Hinogami et al. 2012) and oxalic acid (Kumar et al. 2012). In the latter example, the proximity of two copper sites in the node of a $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ MOF may have served as sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ dimerization to the oxalic acid $\mathbf{C}_{2}$ product (Fig. 3B) A ZIF-8 MOF with catalytically active and accessible $Z\mathrm{n(I)}$ sites was recently utilized towards $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion to CO (Wang et al. 2017) (Fig. 3C). Enhanced selectivity and activity were obtained in the presence of Cl- anions, highlighting the influence of electrolyte ions on MOF conductivity and electrocatalysis. Furthermore, the environment around the Zn node was found to be the essential $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding site because calcined ZIF-8 samples that lost their well-defined coordination environment, as well as ZIF-67 electrodes that featured a Co atom in place of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ , exhibited much lower selectivity for CO.
A Zn-BTC MOF was used as a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ catalyst in an ionic liquid electrolyte (Kang et al. 2016). $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ was the primary product $(>80\%$ selectivity, with CO and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ as minor products). Both the rate and the selectivity of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ conversion were maximized when the imidazolium-derived ionic liquids contained fluorine groups, which presumably facilitated stronger interactions of bound $\mathrm{CO}_{2^{\prime}}$ /intermediates with the ionic liquids. This work exemplifies the use of a MOF to provide discrete active sites for catalysis and the electrolyte to confer secondary sphere interactions that influence reactivity. MOF and COF structures can also be utilized towards reactive environment engineering: COFs with amine linkages were recently synthesized as a thin film on top of catalytically active silver electrodes for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ (Liu et al. 2018). The presence of the COF film increased the selectivity towards CO production by facilitating the formation of a carbamate intermediate.
A $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ MOF was also used as a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture agent (Qiu et al. 2018). In this work, the MOF was mixed with $\mathrm{Cu}$ nanoparticles and loaded into a gas-diffusion electrode. The exposed Cu sites in the MOF functioned as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentrating agents near the surface of the $\mathrm{Cu}$ nanoparticles and increased their selectivity for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ formation while simultaneously suppressing $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ formation. Another study examined the behavior of a ZIF-8 MOF film grown over an array of plasmonic $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanocrystals (Lee et al. 2017). Surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopic (SERS) measurements revealed that the MOF was not in direct
