contact with the Ag, and when $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ flowed through the system at ambient temperature and pressure it formed a liquid-like condensed film at the Ag-MOF interface. No $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ measurements were performed but such composites would be an interesting system to investigate.
Utilizing molecularly-defined active sites in the form of porphyrin linkers, Co and Fe porphyrincontaining MOFs served as the first examples of heterogenized molecular $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ catalysts within a threedimensional MOF skeleton (Hod et al. 2015a, Kornienko et al. 2015) (Fig. 3D). UV-Vis spectroelectrochemical studies of the Co porphyrin MOF illustrated that $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction was initiated upon the reduction of $\mathrm{Co(II)}$ to $\mathrm{Co(I)}$ , and that each molecular linker within the framework was electrochemically accessible. Following these works, a ${\mathrm{Re}}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}$ bipyridine linker-functionalized MOF thin film was found to feature selectivity for CO production in an organic electrolyte, adding to the library of molecularly functional catalytic species used within electroactive MOFs (Ye et al. 2016).
Conductive COFs, also bearing Co porphyrin active sites were utilized as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction electrocatalysts (Lin et al. 2015). Their conductive nature and porosity resulted in a highly efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ catalyst with turnover numbers of ${\sim}3{\times}10^{6}$ and a stability of ${\sim}300\ \mathrm{h}$ . Interestingly, the Co active site within the COF featured much higher activity than the molecular linker on its own. Follow-up XAS experiments and spectral modeling illustrated that inductive effects from functional groups within the COF structure significantly altered the electronics of the Co active site (Diercks et al. 2018). Consequently, the presence of electron withdrawing groups experimentally increased the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathbf{R}$ activity and selectivity of the Co porphyrin units. This work illustrated that the delocalized electronic structure of COFs may be used to modulate the activity on the catalytic sites via inductive effects from species covalently bound to the framework and not necessarily only to the catalyst.
## Future prospects of MOFs and COFs in $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ fixation
The research highlighted above exemplifies several MOF/COF-enabled strategies in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ . Initially, the heterogenization of molecularly-defined catalysts led to the advent of a series of selective and stable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ catalysts. Furthermore, MOFs and COFs have also been utilized as catalytic environment modifiers to modulate reaction energetics and pathways, similar to how secondary sphere effects function within an enzymatic pocket. To progress ahead, there are several promising strategies that can be employed to attain
This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
catalytic functionality not feasible with standard heterogeneous or homogeneous catalysts. In particular, selectively producing further reduced products and $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ species remains a critical challenge.
The chemical diversity of nodes and linkers within MOFs and COFs can be utilized towards the generation of a multi-functional catalytic environment. In this enzyme-like MOF/COF cavity, undercoordinated transition metal species may serve as binding sites while grafted cationic ligands such as imidazolium species function to stabilize high energy anionic intermediates like $\mathrm{CO}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ (Fig. 4A). Much like the functional groups within an enzyme can be “pre-organized” to reduce activation energies, MOF/COF ligands may function the same way. Addional approaches may investigate how catalytic pocket hydrophobicity may play a role in influencing catalytic rates and selectivity. These principles were, in part, demonstrated with homogenous catalysts (Azcarate et al. 2016, Nichols et al. 2018) and would serve as rational starting points for implementation within a MOF/COF framework. Furthermore, the incorporation of proton relays, previously demonstrated for HER catalysis, may promote hydrogenation of intermediates in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ (Hod et al. 2015b).
A similar principle can apply to core-shell MOF-nanocrystal composites in which amine-containing ligands may hydrogen bond with oxygen atoms of intermediates (Fig. 4B). This may promote the stabilization, and longer retention times of such intermediates, which may consequently lead to higher reduced products (e.g. $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ instead of CO) and to dimerization and $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ species. This approach would also allow for selective control of the reactant binding sites (through selection of nanocrystal composition and morphology) and reactive environment (conferred via the surrounding MOF chemistry). Additional avenues towards promoting dimerization of reactants could be the design of catalytic pockets that feature two proximal binding sites where two $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules can first adsorb and be reduced to intermediates that can easily dimerize. By tuning the individual chemistry of each site and consequently the primary adsorbed intermediate, selectivity for a specific $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ products may be attained.
The use of multivariate (MTV) MOFs and COFs can also lead to selective catalysis for highly-reduced products. For example, materials may be grown in an alternating layer-by-layer method that incorporates both catalysts that are active for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO conversion, and sites that are active for CO to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ conversion (Fig. 4C).
