## Vibrational spectroscopy for studying reaction mechanisms within MOFs and COFs
While there are now reports of catalytically active MOFs and COFs, a complete understanding of their reactivity (i.e. reaction mechanisms and factors affecting them), is not yet established. To this end, spectroscopic investigations that can resolve the molecular processes within the electroactive frameworks would be beneficial. Initial studies utilizing in situ UV-Vis spectroelectrochemical techniques on MOFs accommodating Co porphyrin actives sites probed electronic absorption of the porphyrin units (Kornienko et al. 2015). The potential-dependent shift of the Soret band at ${\sim}420~\mathrm{nm}$ upon reduction from $\operatorname{Co}(\operatorname{II})$ to Co(I) was used to track the number of electrically contacted porphyrin active sites and extract the porphyrin’s redox potential (Kornienko et al. 2015). The detection of a $\mathrm{Co(I)}$ porphyrin indicated that this species may serve as the starting point for the catalytic reaction, though the chemical nature of the reaction intermediates remained unresolved.
Vibrational spectroscopy, such as Fourier-transformed infrared (FTIR) and Raman spectroscopy, is generally capable of providing such information. Both techniques directly probe molecular vibrations that exclusively arise from intrinsic properties of the target molecules as well as their interactions with the direct environment. So far, vibrational spectroscopy was mainly employed to monitor macroscopic properties of MOFs/COFs such as structure and stability under varied conditions. The vibrational fingerprints from the materials’ organic building can be used to probe structural alterations (Ciupa and Ptak 2016, Szymborska-Małek et al. 2016) or phase transitions (M czka et al. 2015, Kumari et al. 2016). Moreover, FTIR and Raman spectroscopy have been used for studying interactions/inclusions of small molecules such as hydrogen, water as well as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ within MOFs (Nijem et al. 2011a, Nijem et al. 2011b). In particular, FTIR spectroscopy has been utilized to study $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture in MOFs by probing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ dynamics, interaction modes and binding energies on MOF metal sites (FitzGerald et al. 2015).
In situ FTIR and Raman spectroelectrochemistry have already been successfully applied to study enzymatic reactions (Sezer et al. 2011, Heidary et al. 2015) which feature buried active sites within a well-organized matrix similar to MOFs. These methods were recently used to monitor electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion in heterogenized molecular (Reuillard et al. 2017) and heterogeneous (Wuttig et al. 2016) catalysts. Initial studies with in situ resonance Raman spectroelectrochemistry have probed Co-porphyrin active sites (Kornienko et al. 2015). Combined Raman and time-resolved FTIR studies on ${\mathrm{Re}}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}$
