bipyridine complexes within a MOF network observed signals of the ν(CO) mode of the carbonyl ligands (Easun et al. 2014). These bands were used to track rapid photo-isomerization processes that involve dissociation of one of the carbonyl groups. Probing the ν(CO) modes whose frequencies are sensitive to the electronics at the Re atom points to the possibility of tracking catalytic reactions at such sites.
To accurately track catalysis within MOFs/COFs, tailored spectroelectrochemical experimental design will be required. Specifically, experimental configurations need to enable complementary and, importantly, in situ applications (i.e. under potential control and under standard aqueous conditions). A proposed experimental setup that meets such criteria is illustrated in Fig. 5. To operate under aqueous conditions, FTIR measurements may be carried out in attenuated total reflection (ATR) mode using a Siprism IR-active waveguide which simultaneously functions as the conductive support for the MOF/COF system. The Si interface can be readily modified using the established approaches as mentioned above. At the Si/framework/solvent interface, the IR light, coupled from below, is attenuated and reflected giving rise to an evanescent IR wave with a penetration depth of several hundred nanometers. Complementary collection of Raman signals will be achieved in a standard confocal mode with a $180^{\circ}$ scattering geometry. The MOF-modified waveguides can be mounted into specifically designed spectroelectrochemical cells and used as the working electrode in a typical three-electrode configuration.
Moreover, thin and IR transparent metal layers can be deposited onto waveguide/electrode for using thiolbased SAMs. Furthermore, the metal layers would engender surface-enhanced IR and Raman effects at the framework-metal interface (Wang et al. 2007). This approach would allow for the use of the underlying metal surfaces as electrocatalytic substrate binding sites, and the constructed MOF structures as modulators of the reaction environment. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ reactivity at these active interfaces would be elucidated by surface-enhanced ATR-FTIR and Raman spectroscopy, as already demonstrated for unmodified metal electrode-liquid interfaces (Chernyshova et al. 2018). In conjunction with MOFs that allow for precise control of the catalytic reaction environment, the in situ spectroscopic investigations may provide yet-not-achieved mechanistic information that aid in constructing the blueprints for designing the next class of highly efficient catalytic systems.
## Conclusions
This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
New routes of catalyst development and understanding are necessary to accelerate progress in meeting the central goals of artificial photosynthesis: generating chemical fuels from sunlight. Here, the advent of MOFs and COFs opens up opportunities in the design of catalytic systems. Electroactive bio-inspired frameworks, can uniquely function through reaction-intermediate stabilization, tandem catalytic events, and multifunctional interfaces. To this end, we highlighted the latest achievements in framework catalysts and laid the framework for several promising avenues that should be pursued further. Complementary to this, vibrational spectroscopy can be utilized to elucidate, at a molecular level, the chemical transformations occurring within MOFs, COFs, and their composites. All in all, the exploration of framework materials and the influence that the chemistry within their confined catalytic pockets may confer to catalytic events is an intriguing and rapidly growing field.
