## Figure legend
Fig. 1. Natural and artificial photosynthesis. Nature employs a dual-absorber approach with PSII and PSI serving as the charge separating units. Water is oxidized immediately, while $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation occurs over a longer timescale as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation is decoupled from the light reactions (A). Alternatively, artificial approaches use synthetic materials such as semiconductors to absorb light. Light energy can either be used to simultaneously catalyze reactions or be converted to electricity and utilized in a decoupled approach (B).
Fig. 2. MOF conductivity in an electrochemical setting. Electrons must first transfer from the electrode to the MOF, then hop between redox active units or conduct through the film prior to reaching a reactive site for catalysis.
Fig. 3. Mesoscopic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ catalyst systems (A) and $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{BTC})}_{2}$ (B), ZIF-8 (C), and Co-TCPP (D) MOFs used as electrocatalysts. Cu, Zn, and Al transition metals in the MOF nodes (B-D) are depicted in orange, green, and red polyhedra.
This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
Fig. 4. Strategies for enhancing catalysis within MOFs. Installing binding sites as well as stabilizing ligands within a MOF cavity may decrease activation barriers for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ reactions (A). Similarly, using a MOF to control the reaction environment around a catalytic nanocrystal may function to increase retention time and promote dimerization of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ intermediates to generate $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ species (B). Further reduced products may also be attained with MTV-MOFs featuring binding sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and CO reduction (C).
Fig. 5. Proposed experimental approach that enables the concomitant application of ATR-IR and confocal Raman spectroelectrochemistry to investigate catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{R}$ within MOFs and COFs. The framework is grown on an IR-active waveguide. The IR light is coupled from below and reflected at the prism/framework interface. Exciting laser light for Raman spectroscopy is focused from above using a confocal approach in $180^{\circ}$ scattering geometry. The grown MOF/COF films can be contacted directly or via an underlying conductive transparent thin metal film to function as the working electrode for spectroelectrochemical experiments.
